.TH FACTOR 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
factor \- print the prime factors of integers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B factor
.RI [ number ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B factor
writes the prime factorization of each
.IR number ,
or, with no operands, of each number on standard input.
Standard input is read to the end of the file.
Each line holds any number of numbers, separated by white space, and
blank lines are ignored.
With operands, standard input is not read.
.PP
Each
.I number
is a decimal integer from 0 to
.BR 18446744073709551615 ,
which is 2^64 \- 1.
It may start with a plus sign, and an operand may have white space
before that.
A leading zero does not make a number octal:
.B 010
is ten.
Anything else is invalid: a minus sign, a
.B 0x
prefix, an exponent or a fraction, other text after the digits (even
white space, in an operand), an empty operand, or a value above the
maximum.
.PP
The results are written to standard output in input order, one line per
number.
Each line has the number, a colon, and the prime factors in ascending
order, each after a space.
A prime that divides the number several times is repeated.
A prime is its own only factor, and 0 and 1 have none, so their lines
end after the colon.
The number is written in plain decimal, without a plus sign or leading
zeros.
.PP
On an invalid number,
.B factor
writes a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
The results for the numbers before it are still written, and the
numbers after it are not examined.
The message does not say which number is at fault.
.SH OPTIONS
.B factor
has no options.
Apart from a first argument of
.BR \-\-help ,
an argument that begins with a dash is an invalid number, and
.B \-\-
is not special.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the busybox version and a usage message on standard output, and
exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
In any other position it is an invalid number.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B factor
starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Every applet also reads
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
and
.B BB_UMASK
at startup, and
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR CONEMUPID ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.B LC_CTYPE
when writing to a console.
None of these changes what
.B factor
writes.
They are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including an empty input, or a first argument of
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid number, with a usage message on standard error.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited, found when a write
fails.
No message is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Factor a number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ factor 360
360: 2 2 2 3 3 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Factor the numbers on a line of standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo 91 1001 | factor
91: 7 13
1001: 7 11 13
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the primes up to 30, the numbers whose line has a single factor:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ seq 2 30 | factor | awk \(aqNF == 2 { print $2 }\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the largest prime factor of a number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ factor 600851475143 | awk \(aq{ print $NF }\(aq
6857
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU factor:
.IP \(bu 2
Numbers are limited to 64 bits.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
so no
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-\-exponents ,
or
.BR \-\-version .
.IP \(bu 2
An invalid number ends the program, where GNU factor reports it and
goes on to the next.
.PP
Factoring is by trial division.
The slowest numbers, such as a prime near 2^64 or the product of two
different primes near 2^32, take seconds.
.PP
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
On standard input, a CR before the LF is removed, and a CR elsewhere is
white space.
.PP
Write errors other than a closed pipe are ignored, and so is any error
when the output buffer is flushed at exit: a short result written to a
pipe whose reader has exited is lost, with status 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR busybox (1),
.BR seq (1)
